    "build": "Playtest feedback, round 19",
    "text": "English club names: Alexandra, Wednesday, Orient and Argyle are one-offs, as in real life (the generator keeps at most one of each in the world, and two Harriers); Redworth Alexandra, New Lamwold Wednesday, North Yeothorpe Alexandra and Old Yeowick Alexandra became Redworth Athletic, New Lamwold United, North Yeothorpe Albion and Old Yeowick City. The Intercontinental Cup, every season, mid-season: the continental champions of the season before, with the Asian and African champions meeting first (the African–Asian Cup), the American champions meeting in the Derby of the Americas, the winners playing the Challenger Cup and the European champion joining them in the final. Single matches at neutral grounds. The new World Club Championship format, in the summer of every fourth season (the year before the World Cup): 32 clubs in eight groups of four (a single round robin), then a round of 16, quarter-finals, semi-finals and the final. Places by continent (Europe 12, South America 6, Asia 5, Africa 4, North America 5): the continental champions of the last four seasons first, then by continental points (winners 10, runners-up 7, semi-finalists 5, quarter-finalists 3, a group stage 1, kept four seasons), no more than two clubs of a nation where there is a choice. The draw uses four pots and keeps a continent out of a group (Europe may have two). It has its own calendar days after the playoffs, a tab of the Cups screen with group tables and its history, and a prize fund. Older saves get the Intercontinental Cup and the new format from their next season. A map of the world (since removed; distances, away trips and the longest-trip note stay): `tools/geocode.mjs` gives every club a place from the real slot it sits on (the city's coordinates, which are plain facts; Wikipedia and Wikidata, with a small table of corrections), written to `js/geo.js`; the names stay fictional. Distances (`FM.Lore.km`) feed long away trips (a point of fitness for each 230 km beyond 350, up to 9, for the visitors, and a coach or charter cost for the away club, shown under Away travel in the finances; the longest trip of a league is noted on its table), and local derbies (neighbours within 30 km that had no rival of their own take each other, named for their towns, about 280 rivalries in all). Older glory: every club that existed before the generated seasons has a summary of the years before them: its league titles (years), its seasons in the top flight and its best decade, from its nation's first top-flight season (`FM.Lore.earlyHistory`; no table is stored). Each nation's champions are drawn year by year from how strong each club stood (a fallen giant had its best days at least twenty years before the generated seasons, an oil-backed club had none), no club wins more than a fifth of the seasons, and every Fallen Giant has at least three titles (so Aldcaster Wednesday, founded 1881, has its own). It shows as Honours before the records under every club's honours. Legends with variety: the plain lines (nine before, 60 now) and the stories drawn from a club's past (title, cup, staying up: 14, 10 and 11 wordings) are used least-used first within each nation, so no line is seen more than about a hundred times in a world of 1,000 clubs (the most common was 340); a save from before has its repeated lines rewritten once. Club details for every club (a card on every club's sheet): motto, anthem, mascot, ground nickname, record crowd, record win and heaviest defeat, most famous match (a cup final, title decider or promotion from the club's own generated past), named eras (the Golden Years, the Wilderness Years, the Cup Years, `The <legend's surname> Years`), and what kind of club it is. Rivalries with an origin: every derby has where it began (the title race, the cup final or the relegation both clubs shared in the generated seasons, the town they share, a feud) and the record between the two clubs before the save. Competition histories: each league, cup and continental competition has a History fold-out (founded, its trophy and the person who gave it, changes of format, the most titles, the giant-killings of the generated cup finals; the oldest cup in the world is marked), also on the Intercontinental and World Club Championships. Special club types are named and explained: military, police and security, railway, works and company, trade-union, university and reserve sides show a badge beside the club's identity and a paragraph on the club card (\"Players serve here during national service\" for Bujeong Garrison). The world's people: former players of the generated past are now in the game: about one club in ten has a legend of its own or of its rival as manager (named on the opposition card for the match, with a line when he faces his old club), one club in five has a legend as chairman or president, three in ten have an ambassador, and three great names of the big clubs join the Touchline Tonight panel. Legends' own cards say what they do now.",
