    "build": "Playtest feedback, round 18",
    "text": "Proper restarts in the match view: after a goal and at half-time the sides walk back to their own halves, the side that did not score keeps outside the centre circle, and the ball is played from the centre spot (the second half is kicked off by the other side). A striker played in by a through ball or on a counter takes it toward goal before he shoots. A small shirt in each side's kit colour beside its name at the top of the match screen. Goal kicks and throw-ins on the pitch, with their share and landing places from the Wyscout data (`npm run carries`); a defensive wall for direct free kicks; free-kick crosses with the box filling as for a corner; carries fitted to the DFL open tracking (7 Bundesliga matches) and the PFF World Cup tracking (64 matches) at equal weight: about 34% of receptions are followed by a carry of 4 m or more, median 6.7 m (`npm run carries:tracking`), take-ons (about 26 a match, a win takes the carrier past, a loss gives the defender the ball) and short touches (1 to 4 m, after about half of receptions) from the real data. The StatsBomb open data (360 frames, `npm run layouts`) places the players at corners, throw-ins and goal kicks as real ones stand, sizes the free-kick wall by distance (6, 4.6, 2 and 1.2 men), and adds a third measure of carries and take-ons. The PFF World Cup events (`npm run events:pff`) set the goal kicks (about 13 a match) and throw-ins (about 38) of the match view and the pass accuracy of the match stats. Fewer unrealistic passes: no pass back to the keeper from the opponent's half, fewer passes back in that half, a pass into the box with no shot behind it is usually lost, and the ball is not passed around inside the box. Carriers past halfway dribble further and more often. The first Analyst view slice, in the post-match Shots & xG tab: the match in numbers, an xG timeline with each goal's scorer, minute and score, red cards and substitutions marked, a chart of who was threatening minute by minute, and each side's best shooters. The Analyst profile card on every player's sheet: per-90 percentiles against his position, a written summary, a shot map and the three players most like him; the engine records expected goals, expected assists and shot places for every player in every simulated match. The Analyst profile card on every club's sheet: its league season per match ranked among the league's clubs, a style radar, a written summary, the formations used, the run of its expected goals and (for your own club) where its chances come from. League charts: a Charts tab on the league screen with five scatter plots (chances for and against, goals against expected, possession against chance quality, pressing against possession, and every scorer's goals against expected goals), your club ringed and a tap opening any club or player. Tools to build a pack of your own: `npm run realplayers:fetch` and `realplayers:build` read openly licensed squads (Wikipedia, Wikidata) and write a world pack in the game's own format, with ratings estimated from how well known each player is, to a private folder outside the repository (`tools/private.mjs`). The game ships no pack and no real names.",
