    "build": "Playtest feedback, round 17",
    "text": "The Nations League replaces the international friendlies: each confederation plays one in the seasons with no qualifying (the year with no tournament and the year of the Continental Champions Trophy), in leagues of sixteen nations and groups of four, on the first three matchdays of the breaks. Group winners go up, the last in each group go down, and the best League A group winner is champion. A nation with nothing to play in a window rests. Confederations in the world editor: International football → Confederations lists the five and any you add, and moves a nation between them. A confederation sets qualifying, the Nations League and the continental championship; one with four nations or more gets a World Championship place and a championship of its own. Prize money for leagues, cups and competitions: a league pays by final position from a fund the editor can set (automatic is a part of each club's revenue), a domestic cup pays its fund down the rounds, a continental or world cup pays per win and for the title; the Finances tab lists what the club won, the cups and league screens show the funds. A currency picker in Settings with 38 currencies at fixed rates (Auto follows the club's country). Catalonia and the Basque Country can be added as nations (new-career screen and the world editor): players of Catalan or Basque family can choose them, and each fields a national team with its own flag. Dropping down in the editor: a continental cup can send the clubs that finish third in a group into the cup one tier below, to play its group runners-up in a knockout play-off before the quarter-finals (tier 1 into tier 2, tier 2 into tier 3). The visual match engine's positioning comes from real tracking data: a shape table built from SkillCorner Open Data (MIT) by `npm run shapes` moves each position with the ball, with and without it, and the lines are drawn together as in real teams. The rest of the realism plan (passes, shots, set pieces, movement) is still to do. Shot locations and xG from real data: each chance is a real shot from the Wyscout public event data (46,000 shots; the model fits distance, angle, headers and free kicks, and matches the observed goal rate at every distance), with each kind's average chance kept so goals per match do not move. The match view's lines between players are gone. Pass choice from real data with an expected-threat grid: 1.65 million Wyscout passes give the share of passes that go each way from each part of the pitch and an xT grid (best cell 41%, the penalty area's centre 16%, our own box 1%); the match view picks receivers by them, with the style on top (Direct 29 m a pass, Possession 21 m). The shape of real passing is only partly matched (47% forward against 57% in the data), because the formations' geometry limits it. Real speeds in the match view from the Metrica Sports sample data: top speed by pace and the real spread of speeds, a limit on how fast a player can speed up, slow down or turn, and pass flight times from real ball speeds by length. The pitch is not drawn at real time (a match minute lasts about a second), so metres a second are scaled; players still move a little more than real ones (a median of about 2.6 m/s against 1.5). Set pieces from real data: 19,316 corners give where deliveries go (near post, middle, far post, the edge of the area, short), how often a corner ends in a shot (27%) and a goal (2.5%), and the first touch (attackers 43%, defenders 46%); free kicks give what is done from each distance (a shot from inside 25 m, mostly crosses from 30 to 40 m). The engine takes one real corner at a time, the view puts the ball at the flag, crowds the box and plays short corners, and the commentary names the zone. A goal replay in the developer tools (since removed, with the tracking data it used): tracked real goals beside goals recorded from the match view. First finding: real goals come from a crowded area (3.3 attackers and 6.4 defenders in the area at the shot on average) and the match view's was open (0.8 and 2.9); a minute that ends in a shot now sends attackers into the area from its start and the defending side back, giving 3.1 and 6.3.",
